    "['sysname [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'hostname [parameter1]', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'hostname [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['clock ssm-control [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'clock source ssm enable', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'clock source ssm enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nclock source ssm enable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\nIn Cisco IOS, `clock source ssm enable` is the equivalent command to Huawei's `clock ssm-control on`. Both commands enable the Synchronization Status Message (SSM) control for clock synchronization.\\n\\nThe SSM feature is used to select the best quality clock source available in a synchronous network by exchanging quality level information between nodes.\", 'trans_template': 'clock source ssm enable'}]]": 1,
    "['clock bits-type [parameter1] [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'clock source bits0 2mbps', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'clock source bits0 2mbps', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nclock source bits0 2mbps\\n##  \\n\\nNote:  \\n1. Cisco\\'s equivalent command uses \"clock source\" instead of \"clock bits-type\"  \\n2. The interface name (bits0) and rate (2mbps) remain the same  \\n3. This configures the BITS0 interface as a 2Mbps synchronization source in Cisco IOS, matching the Huawei functionality', 'trans_template': 'clock source bits0 2mbps'}]]": 1,
    "['clock source [parameter1] synchronization [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'clock source external 0/0/0 input', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'clock source external 0/0/0 input', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nclock source external 0/0/0 input\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, the equivalent command enables an external clock source (typically on a controller interface). The \"bits0\" in Huawei maps to an external timing interface in Cisco (often specified as 0/0/0 or similar). The command enables synchronization from this source, analogous to Huawei\\'s configuration. The exact interface may vary based on Cisco platform.', 'trans_template': 'clock source external 0/0/0 input'}]]": 1,
    "['clock source [parameter1] priority [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network-clock input-source 1 bits0', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network-clock input-source 1 bits0', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nnetwork-clock input-source 1 bits0\\n##  \\n\\nNote: In Cisco, the concept of \"priority\" for clock sources is typically handled by the order of input sources (where lower numbered sources have higher priority). The command `network-clock input-source 1 bits0` sets `bits0` as the primary input source (priority 1), as Cisco doesn\\'t use a direct \"priority\" keyword like Huawei. If multiple sources are configured, their order determines the priority.', 'trans_template': 'network-clock input-source 1 bits0'}]]": 1,
    "['interface [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'interface [parameter1]', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]'}]]": 2,
    "['clock synchronization [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network-clock synchronization enable', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network-clock synchronization enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nnetwork-clock synchronization enable\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\n- The Huawei `clock synchronization enable` command enables clock synchronization on an interface or clock source.\\n- The equivalent Cisco command is `network-clock synchronization enable`, which similarly enables network clock synchronization for timing distribution.\\n- The functionality is essentially the same, just with Cisco's different syntax for clock-related configurations.\", 'trans_template': 'network-clock synchronization enable'}]]": 1,
    "['clock synchronization [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network-clock synchronization enable', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'network-clock synchronization enable', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nnetwork-clock synchronization enable\\n##  \\n\\nNote:  \\n1. In Cisco, the equivalent command for clock synchronization is `network-clock synchronization enable` (used on global configuration mode or interface level).  \\n2. If this needs to be applied to a specific interface (like the Huawei example), the Cisco equivalent would typically involve configuring synchronous Ethernet (SyncE) with:  \\n   ```\\n   interface GigabitEthernet1/0/0  \\n    network-clock synchronization enable  \\n   ```  \\n   However, since the original request was for the standalone command, the global version is provided.  \\n3. The `clock synchronization enable` command in Huawei enables synchronization on an interface, while Cisco uses `network-clock` terminology for similar functionality.', 'trans_template': 'network-clock synchronization enable'}]]": 1,
    "['clock priority [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'network-clock-select 1 priority 3', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nnetwork-clock-select 1 priority 3\\n##  \\n\\n(Note: In Cisco, the equivalent command for setting clock priority is typically under `network-clock-select` hierarchy, where \"1\" represents the clock source line. The priority value \"3\" remains the same.)', 'trans_template': 'network-clock-select 1 priority 3'}]]": 1,
